  section "Local Measures"

    measure @ashford-prop-a "Proposition A"
      subtitle "School improvement bond"
      description:
        Shall the measure titled "School improvement bond" be adopted, authorizing the actions
        described in the full text and amending the relevant code sections accordingly?
      if_yes:
        The school improvement bond would take effect as described.
      if_no:
        Current law would remain unchanged.
      tabulation "Simple majority"
      fiscal_impact "$48 per parcel per year for 15 years"
      supporters "Sierra Club, Franklin Chapter", "Franklin Teachers Association", "Franklin Realtors Association"
      opponents "Franklin Realtors Association"

      recommend yes
        justification:
          Two of our board members work for organizations that would receive funding under this
          measure. They recused themselves and the remaining members were evenly split.

    measure @ashford-prop-b "Proposition B"
      subtitle "Health care, shelter, and street safety bond"
      placement "Veto referendum"
      tabulation "Requires two-thirds of votes cast"
      fiscal_impact "$48 per parcel per year for 20 years"

      recommend yes
        justification:
          This is a straightforward bond for infrastructure the district has deferred for a
          decade. The repayment schedule is conservative and the oversight committee has real
          teeth.

    measure @ashford-prop-c "Proposition C"
      subtitle "Inspector general"
      tabulation "Majority of all registered voters"
      fiscal_impact "About $48 million a year for 20 years"
      supporters "Millbrook Neighborhood Council", "Franklin Nurses Union"
      opponents none

      recommend no-position
        justification:
          The mechanism here is the problem, not the goal. It sets no limits on what localities
          may do in its place, and the cities most eager to use it have the worst records on the
          underlying issue.

          We would support a narrower version. Vote no on this one.

    measure @ashford-prop-d "Proposition D"
      subtitle "City commissions and mayoral authority"
      description:
        Shall the measure titled "City commissions and mayoral authority" be adopted,
        authorizing the actions described in the full text and amending the relevant code
        sections accordingly?
      if_yes:
        The city commissions and mayoral authority would take effect as described.
      if_no:
        Current law would remain unchanged.
      placement "Placed by the City Council"
      tabulation "Majority of all registered voters"
      fiscal_impact "No direct fiscal effect"
      opponents "Franklin Taxpayers Association", "Franklin Police Officers Association"
      note:
        Propositions D and E are mutually exclusive. If both pass, the one with more votes
        becomes law and the other is discarded.

      recommend yes
        justification:
          This is a straightforward bond for infrastructure the district has deferred for a
          decade. The repayment schedule is conservative and the oversight committee has real
          teeth.

    measure @ashford-prop-e "Proposition E"
      subtitle "Commission streamlining task force"
      tabulation "Simple majority"
      fiscal_impact "Authorizes $140 million in bonds, repaid by property taxes over 10 years"
      supporters "Franklin Housing Coalition"
      note:
        Propositions D and E are mutually exclusive. If both pass, the one with more votes
        becomes law and the other is discarded.

      recommend no
        justification:
          Enshrines in the charter a right the courts have already recognized. Cheap, symbolic,
          and worth doing.

    measure @ashford-prop-f "Proposition F"
      subtitle "Deferred police retirement"
      placement "Required by the City Charter"
      tabulation "Requires two-thirds of votes cast"
      fiscal_impact "About $300 million a year in new revenue"
      opponents none

      recommend yes
        justification:
          This is a straightforward bond for infrastructure the district has deferred for a
          decade. The repayment schedule is conservative and the oversight committee has real
          teeth.

    measure @ashford-prop-g "Proposition G"
      subtitle "Rental subsidies for affordable housing"
      description:
        Shall the measure titled "Rental subsidies for affordable housing" be adopted,
        authorizing the actions described in the full text and amending the relevant code
        sections accordingly?
      if_yes:
        The rental subsidies for affordable housing would take effect as described.
      if_no:
        Current law would remain unchanged.
      tabulation "Majority of all registered voters"
      fiscal_impact "About $30 million a year in new revenue"
      supporters "Franklin Teachers Association", "Franklin Small Business Network"
      opponents "Franklin Nurses Union"

      recommend yes
        justification:
          Consolidates six overlapping programs into one with a single budget. It should cut
          overhead and make oversight possible for the first time.

    measure @ashford-prop-h "Proposition H"
      subtitle "Firefighter retirement age"
      placement "Placed by the District Board"
      tabulation "Requires 55%"
      fiscal_impact "Authorizes $30 million in bonds, repaid by property taxes over 10 years"

      recommend yes
        justification:
          The existing subsidy is not enough and this modest increase is a cheap fix. It will
          not solve the underlying shortage, but it is better than nothing.

    measure @ashford-prop-i "Proposition I"
      subtitle "Retirement benefits for nurses and dispatchers"
      tabulation "Majority of all registered voters"
      fiscal_impact "No direct fiscal effect"
      supporters "Franklin Housing Coalition"
      opponents none

      recommend yes
        justification:
          This is a straightforward bond for infrastructure the district has deferred for a
          decade. The repayment schedule is conservative and the oversight committee has real
          teeth.

    measure @ashford-prop-j "Proposition J"
      subtitle "Children and youth fund consolidation"
      description:
        Shall the measure titled "Children and youth fund consolidation" be adopted, authorizing
        the actions described in the full text and amending the relevant code sections
        accordingly?
      if_yes:
        The children and youth fund consolidation would take effect as described.
      if_no:
        Current law would remain unchanged.
      placement "Placed by the City Council"
      tabulation "Majority of all registered voters"
      fiscal_impact "$48 per parcel per year for 20 years"
      opponents "Franklin Realtors Association"

      recommend yes
        justification:
          Brings the retirement age back to where it was in 2012. The staffing picture here is
          nothing like the shortage elsewhere, and the health-risk argument is real.

    measure @ashford-prop-k "Proposition K"
      subtitle "Close the Upper Shore Highway to cars"
      tabulation "Simple majority"
      fiscal_impact "Authorizes $140 million in bonds, repaid by property taxes over 10 years"
      supporters "Transit Riders Alliance", "Franklin Small Business Network", "Franklin Police Officers Association"

      recommend yes
        justification:
          This creates a new office with no new powers and no clear mandate. It is redundant
          with three existing bodies and adds a layer of coordination at best.

    measure @ashford-prop-l "Proposition L"
      subtitle "Ride-hail tax for transit"
      placement "Placed by the City Council"
      tabulation "Requires two-thirds of votes cast"
      fiscal_impact "About $300 million a year for 10 years"
      opponents none

      recommend no-position
        justification:
          A flat per-parcel tax is regressive, but the alternative is cutting the service
          entirely, and the people who depend on it most are the ones a cut would hurt.

    measure @ashford-prop-m "Proposition M"
      subtitle "Business tax restructuring"
      description:
        Shall the measure titled "Business tax restructuring" be adopted, authorizing the
        actions described in the full text and amending the relevant code sections accordingly?
      if_yes:
        The business tax restructuring would take effect as described.
      if_no:
        Current law would remain unchanged.
      tabulation "Majority of all registered voters"
      fiscal_impact "About $30 million a year for 15 years"
      supporters "Franklin Small Business Network", "Transit Riders Alliance", "Franklin Teachers Association"
      opponents "Franklin Nurses Union", "Franklin Taxpayers Association"

      recommend yes
        justification:
          The existing subsidy is not enough and this modest increase is a cheap fix. It will
          not solve the underlying shortage, but it is better than nothing.

    measure @ashford-prop-n "Proposition N"
      subtitle "First responder student loan forgiveness"
      placement "Veto referendum"
      tabulation "Requires two-thirds of votes cast"
      fiscal_impact "No direct fiscal effect"

      recommend yes
        justification:
          Two of our board members work for organizations that would receive funding under this
          measure. They recused themselves and the remaining members were evenly split.

    measure @ashford-prop-o "Proposition O"
      subtitle "Reproductive freedom"
      tabulation "Requires two-thirds of votes cast"
      fiscal_impact "$300 per parcel per year for 30 years"
      supporters "Franklin Nurses Union", "Sierra Club, Franklin Chapter", "Franklin Small Business Network"
      opponents none

      recommend yes
        justification:
          Brings the retirement age back to where it was in 2012. The staffing picture here is
          nothing like the shortage elsewhere, and the health-risk argument is real.

